Text 2: Researchers fitted employees at two firms with sensors before and after a move to open-plan layouts. Face-to-face interaction fell by roughly 70 percent, while email and messaging volume rose sharply.', 'Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the argument in Text 1?', '[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing with it and offering the sensor data as additional support"},{"key":"B","text":"By arguing that collaboration is impossible to measure in any workplace"},{"key":"C","text":"By pointing out that their measurements show the opposite of the predicted effect"},{"key":"D","text":"By noting that the effect appears only in firms of a certain size"}]'::jsonb, 'C', null, 'Text 1 predicts more in-person collaboration; Text 2 measured a large fall in face-to-face interaction, which directly contradicts that prediction. Choice D invents a limitation the passage never mentions.') on conflict (id) do nothing;

Text 2: Since receiving several returned bronzes, a national museum has hosted visiting researchers from eleven countries and published a catalogue that scholars abroad had been unable to compile from photographs alone.', 'Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to the concern raised in Text 1?', '[{"key":"A","text":"It confirms the concern by showing that scholarship slowed after the return."},{"key":"B","text":"It restates the concern in more concrete terms."},{"key":"C","text":"It dismisses the concern as irrelevant to museums outside Europe."},{"key":"D","text":"It qualifies the concern by showing that returned objects can still support comparative scholarship."}]'::jsonb, 'D', null, 'Text 1 worries that returns will fragment scholarly access; Text 2 shows access continuing, and improving, after a return — which softens the concern without declaring it baseless. Choice C overstates: Text 2 offers evidence rather than dismissal.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
• The Tigray region has more than 120 recorded rock-hewn churches.
• Many are carved into cliff faces reachable only on foot.
• Their construction dates are debated, ranging from the 6th to the 15th century.
• Conservation surveys began in earnest only in the 1990s.', 'The student wants to emphasise the difficulty of studying these churches. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"key":"A","text":"The Tigray region has more than 120 recorded rock-hewn churches, some dating to the 6th century."},{"key":"B","text":"Tigray''s rock-hewn churches were carved into cliff faces, and there are more than 120 of them."},{"key":"C","text":"Conservation surveys of the Tigray churches began in the 1990s, and more than 120 churches have been recorded."},{"key":"D","text":"Reachable only on foot and surveyed systematically just since the 1990s, Tigray''s rock-hewn churches have construction dates still debated across nine centuries."}]'::jsonb, 'D', null, 'The goal is difficulty of study, and only choice D combines the obstacles — hard access, late start, and unresolved dating. The other choices report facts accurately but none of them frames anything as an impediment.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
• Mycorrhizal fungi attach to plant roots.
• The fungi supply phosphorus to the plant.
• The plant supplies sugars to the fungi.
• Plants grown without the fungi in phosphorus-poor soil grow about 40% smaller.', 'The student wants to present the relationship as mutually beneficial. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Mycorrhizal fungi attach to plant roots, where they supply phosphorus."},{"key":"B","text":"Plants grown without mycorrhizal fungi in phosphorus-poor soil grow about 40% smaller."},{"key":"C","text":"Mycorrhizal fungi supply phosphorus to the plants they attach to, and receive sugars in return."},{"key":"D","text":"Mycorrhizal fungi and plants are both found in phosphorus-poor soils."}]'::jsonb, 'C', null, 'Mutual benefit requires both directions of exchange, and only choice C states what each partner gives and gets. Choice A describes only one direction, and choice B shows a cost of absence rather than a two-way benefit.') on conflict (id) do nothing;

Text 2: In a trial across 60 villages, households receiving cash reported better outcomes than those receiving goods on almost every measure. The exception was vaccination rates, which rose only where a clinic was also funded.', 'Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 refutes the argument in Text 1 by showing that cash transfers rarely help."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 supports Text 1 by showing that vaccination rates rose wherever cash was given."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 largely supports Text 1 while identifying an outcome that cash alone did not improve."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 is unrelated to the argument in Text 1 because it studies villages rather than agencies."}]'::jsonb, 'C', null, 'Cash won on almost every measure, which supports the argument, but vaccination required a funded clinic, which marks a limit. Choice B misreads that exception as a confirmation.') on conflict (id) do nothing;

Text 2: A biographer notes that in three separate letters the novelist described the ending she had discarded, and that knowing this makes an otherwise puzzling final chapter legible.', 'The biographer in Text 2 would most likely respond to Text 1 by arguing that', '[{"key":"A","text":"letters are always more reliable than novels as evidence of meaning."},{"key":"B","text":"the scholar in Text 1 has misread the final chapter of the novel."},{"key":"C","text":"a novelist''s discarded endings are of no interest to literary critics."},{"key":"D","text":"external documents can resolve difficulties that the fiction alone leaves unexplained."}]'::jsonb, 'D', null, 'Text 2''s case rests on the letters making a puzzling chapter legible, which is a claim about usefulness, not about general authority. Choice A overstates it into a blanket ranking the biographer never makes.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
• The Great Ethiopian Run was first held in 2001.
• It attracts more than 40,000 participants annually.
• Elite and amateur runners compete on the same 10 km course.
• Registration fees subsidise youth athletics programmes.', 'The student wants to emphasise the event''s scale. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"key":"A","text":"The Great Ethiopian Run, first held in 2001, subsidises youth athletics through its registration fees."},{"key":"B","text":"More than 40,000 runners take part in the Great Ethiopian Run each year, with elite and amateur competitors sharing a single 10 km course."},{"key":"C","text":"The Great Ethiopian Run is a 10 km race that was first held in 2001."},{"key":"D","text":"Registration fees for the Great Ethiopian Run support youth athletics programmes across the country."}]'::jsonb, 'B', null, 'Scale is a matter of size, and only choice B leads with the 40,000 participants and the shared course. The other options are accurate but foreground funding or founding date instead.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
• Lake Chad shrank by about 90% between 1963 and the 1990s.
• Irrigation withdrawals increased sharply over the same period.
• Rainfall in the basin also declined.
• Since 2000 the lake''s area has been broadly stable.', 'The student wants to explain that the lake''s decline had more than one cause. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '[{"key":"A","text":"Lake Chad shrank by about 90% between 1963 and the 1990s."},{"key":"B","text":"Since 2000, the area of Lake Chad has been broadly stable."},{"key":"C","text":"Lake Chad''s sharp decline coincided with both a rise in irrigation withdrawals and a fall in rainfall across the basin."},{"key":"D","text":"Irrigation withdrawals from Lake Chad increased sharply between 1963 and the 1990s."}]'::jsonb, 'C', null, 'Multiple causes require naming more than one factor, and only choice C pairs irrigation with declining rainfall. Choices A and D each state a single element of the picture.') on conflict (id) do nothing;
